Rem ==================================================================
Rem https.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: an HTTP client over TLS
Rem
Rem Built on net.shoddy, which supplies the sockets and the TLS upgrade.
Rem This machine is the HTTP part: building a request, and taking a
Rem reply apart.
Rem
Rem     Let reply = HttpsGet("api.weather.gov", "/points/41.88,-87.63",
Rem         { Pair("User-Agent", "my-mill (me@example.com)"),
Rem           Pair("Accept", "application/geo+json") })
Rem     If HttpStatus(reply) = 200 Then
Rem         Print(HttpBody(reply))
Rem
Rem PURE WHERE IT CAN BE, and that is the design rule. Building a request
Rem and parsing a reply are functions on Strings: HttpRequestText,
Rem HttpStatus, HttpHeaders and HttpBody touch no socket, need no
Rem --allow-net, and are tested offline against fixture text. Only
Rem HttpsGet and HttpsRequest reach the network.
Rem
Rem Headers are a List Of Pair — dict.shoddy's association list — so
Rem DictGetOr works on a parsed reply without any help from here:
Rem
Rem     Let hs = HttpHeaders(reply)
Rem     Print(DictGetOr(hs, "content-type", "unknown"))
Rem
Rem Names are LOWERCASED on the way in. HTTP header names are
Rem case-insensitive, and normalising at the boundary is what lets that
Rem DictGetOr just work rather than having to guess whether this
Rem particular server wrote Content-Type or content-type.
Rem
Rem ---- known limits ------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem HTTP/1.0, deliberately. It is what makes "the peer closes when it has
Rem finished" the correct end-of-body rule, so there is no chunked
Rem decoder here and none is needed: a server answering HTTP/1.0 does not
Rem send Transfer-Encoding: chunked. Speaking 1.1 would buy keep-alive
Rem and cost a chunk decoder, a Content-Length reader and a connection
Rem cache — a fair trade one day, and not this machine.
Rem
Rem No redirects are followed. A 301 or 302 is reported, not chased:
Rem chasing one means deciding about loops, cross-host hops and method
Rem rewriting, and those are the caller's policy rather than a library's.
Rem HttpStatus and the location header are right there.
Rem
Rem No compression. Nothing here sends Accept-Encoding, so nothing
Rem arrives gzipped. Do not add the header without adding a decoder.
Rem
Rem The body is BYTES, one per character, exactly as ReadFile hands them
Rem over. A UTF-8 page therefore arrives as its bytes: writing it back
Rem out with WriteFile reproduces the file exactly, and turning it into
Rem characters is a separate concern this machine does not have.
Rem
Rem Certificate validation is the platform's, and a failure aborts the
Rem program — see net.shoddy and the errors page.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "net.shoddy"
Include "dict.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- constants -------------------------------------------------------
Rem Machines cannot carry a top-level Let, so the constants are zero-arg
Rem Defs, as vt100's Esc() is.

Def HttpsPort() As Number
    443

Rem CR LF, and the blank line that ends a header block. Written out of
Rem Chr rather than as escapes so the bytes are unmistakable.
Def HttpEol() As String
    Chr(13) & Chr(10)

Def HttpGap() As String
    HttpEol() & HttpEol()

Rem ---- building a request ----------------------------------------------

Rem The request text, exactly as it goes on the wire. Pure.
Rem
Rem Host is emitted first and always, because HTTP/1.0 servers behind a
Rem name-based vhost need it and it costs nothing to be sure. Anything
Rem else is yours: a User-Agent is worth sending on principle and is
Rem required in practice by more hosts than you would expect — some
Rem answer 403 to a request without one.
Def HttpRequestText(method As String, path As String, host As String, headers As List Of Pair) As String
    Join(Append(Prepend(Upper(method) & " " & path & " HTTP/1.0",
                        Prepend("Host: " & host,
                                Map(headers, Fn(h) => Fst(h) & ": " & Snd(h)))),
                HttpEol()),
         HttpEol())

Rem ---- reading a reply -------------------------------------------------

Rem The status line's number, or 0 if this does not look like a reply at
Rem all. Pure. Val is lax, so the slice is checked before it is trusted:
Rem "HTTP/1.1 200 OK" -> 200.
Def HttpStatus(reply As String) As Number
    Let line = HttpStatusLine(reply)
    Let parts = Filter(Split(line, " "), Fn(p) => p <> "")
    If Length(parts) < 2 Or Not StartsWith(Upper(line), "HTTP/") Then
        0
    Else
        If IsNumeric(Nth(parts, 2)) Then
            Val(Nth(parts, 2))
        Else
            0

Def HttpStatusLine(reply As String) As String
    First(Split(HttpHead(reply), Chr(10)))

Rem The reason phrase — "OK", "Not Found" — or "" if there is none.
Def HttpReason(reply As String) As String
    Let line = HttpTrimCr(HttpStatusLine(reply))
    Let sp1 = Instr(line, " ")
    If sp1 = 0 Then
        ""
    Else
        Let rest = Mid(line, sp1 + 1, Len(line))
        Let sp2 = Instr(rest, " ")
        If sp2 = 0 Then
            ""
        Else
            Mid(rest, sp2 + 1, Len(rest))

Rem Every header, names lowercased and values trimmed, in the order the
Rem server sent them. Pure. The status line is dropped; a line with no
Rem colon is skipped rather than guessed at.
Def HttpHeaders(reply As String) As List Of Pair
    Map(Filter(Rest(Split(HttpHead(reply), Chr(10))), HttpIsHeaderLine),
        HttpHeaderPair)

Def HttpIsHeaderLine(line As String) As Boolean
    Instr(line, ":") > 0

Def HttpHeaderPair(line As String) As Pair
    Let c = Instr(line, ":")
    Pair(Lower(HttpTrimCr(Left(line, c - 1))),
         HttpTrimCr(Trim(Mid(line, c + 1, Len(line)))))

Rem One header by name, or your default. The name you ask with is
Rem lowercased for you, so HttpHeader(r, "Content-Type") works.
Def HttpHeader(reply As String, name As String, dflt As String) As String
    DictGetOr(HttpHeaders(reply), Lower(name), dflt)

Rem Everything after the first blank line. Pure. A bare LF gap is
Rem tolerated, because not every server is careful; "" when there is no
Rem gap at all, which is what a truncated reply looks like.
Def HttpBody(reply As String) As String
    Let g = Instr(reply, HttpGap())
    If g > 0 Then
        Mid(reply, g + 4, Len(reply))
    Else
        Let g2 = Instr(reply, Chr(10) & Chr(10))
        If g2 > 0 Then
            Mid(reply, g2 + 2, Len(reply))
        Else
            ""

Rem The header block, without the body: everything before the first
Rem blank line, or the whole thing if there is no blank line.
Def HttpHead(reply As String) As String
    Let g = Instr(reply, HttpGap())
    If g > 0 Then
        Left(reply, g - 1)
    Else
        Let g2 = Instr(reply, Chr(10) & Chr(10))
        If g2 > 0 Then
            Left(reply, g2 - 1)
        Else
            reply

Rem str's Trim takes spaces only, so a line split on LF keeps its CR.
Def HttpTrimCr(s As String) As String
    If Len(s) > 0 And Right(s, 1) = Chr(13) Then
        Left(s, Len(s) - 1)
    Else
        s

Rem ---- fetching --------------------------------------------------------
Rem The only effectful words here. Everything above is pure.

Rem One GET over TLS, returning the whole raw reply — status line,
Rem headers and body — for the words above to take apart.
Def HttpsGet(host As String, path As String, headers As List Of Pair) As String
    HttpsRequest("GET", host, path, headers)

Rem Any method, same shape. The body of a POST goes in headers-then-text
Rem territory that HTTP/1.0 makes awkward, so this sends none: it is the
Rem general form of a request without one.
Def HttpsRequest(method As String, host As String, path As String, headers As List Of Pair) As String
    RequestTls(host, HttpsPort(),
        HttpRequestText(method, path, host, headers))
